  "final_report": "[stop_reason: hop_cap_reached | hops used: 4/4]\n\nAnalyst summary:\n**Investigation\u202fsummary \u2013 Korea Electric Power Corp. (KEPCO) disruption, September\u202f2011**\n\n| Tier | Company (down\u2011stream or closely linked) | Why it appears at risk* | Evidence found |\n|------|------------------------------------------|------------------------|----------------|\n| 1 | **POSCO** (steel producer, South\u202fKorea) | Listed as a tier\u20111 downstream customer in the supply\u2011graph \u2013 POSCO purchases large volumes of electricity for its steel mills. | \u2022 *Search\u2011corpus* result\u202f(8) shows KEPCO\u2019s September\u202f15\u202f2011 rolling blackouts \u201caffecting ~2\u202fmillion households **and industrial users**.<br>\u2022 No specific mention of POSCO in its own filing (POSCO 20\u2011F, 24\u202fJun\u202f2011). |\n| 1 | **Hyundai Motor** (passenger\u2011vehicle manufacturer) | Tier\u20111 downstream \u2013 automotive plants are heavy electricity consumers. | Same KEPCO event description as above; no company\u2011specific filing found. |\n| 1 | **Kia Corp.** (passenger\u2011vehicle manufacturer) | Tier\u20111 downstream \u2013 similar electricity demand to Hyundai Motor. | Same KEPCO event description; no company\u2011specific filing found. |\n| 1 | **LS\u202fElectric** (electrical\u2011equipment maker) | Tier\u20111 downstream \u2013 supplies and uses high\u2011voltage equipment that depends on stable grid power. | Same KEPCO event description; no company\u2011specific filing found. |\n| 1 | **Samsung C&T** (engineering & construction, materials trading) | Tier\u20111 downstream \u2013 involved in EPC projects that require continuous power. | Same KEPCO event description; no company\u2011specific filing found. |\n| 1 | **Hyundai Engineering\u202f&\u202fConstruction** (EPC) | Tier\u20111 downstream \u2013 builds and operates plants that need reliable electricity. | Same KEPCO event description; no company\u2011specific filing found. |\n| 2 | **Kumho Petrochemical**, **Hansol Chemical**, **Dongsung Chemical**, **OCI**, **LG\u202fElectronics**, **Samsung\u202fSDI**, **SK\u202fOn** (battery maker), **Hyundai\u202fRotem**, **Renault**, **Volvo\u202fAB**, **Mercedes\u2011Benz**, **Ford Motor**, **Bridgestone**, **Kumho Tire**, **Schaeffler**, etc. | Tier\u20112 companies are either secondary suppliers to the tier\u20111 firms above or other industrial users that draw power from KEPCO\u2019s grid. | Same KEPCO event description (rolling blackouts affecting industrial users). No individual filings mention the September\u202f2011 outage. |\n\n\\* *\u201cWhy it appears at risk\u201d* is derived solely from the **traverse_supply_graph** call (down\u2011stream mode, max_tier\u202f=\u202f2). The tool enumerates companies that are structurally linked to KEPCO either as direct electricity customers (tier\u202f1) or as downstream users of those tier\u20111 firms (tier\u202f2).\n\n### Evidence supporting the disruption\n\n* **Event description** \u2013 A free\u2011text search of all filings (`search_corpus`) returned a specific entry dated **15\u202fSep\u202f2011**:  \n\n  > \u201cA late\u2011summer heatwave combined with reactor outages forced KEPCO into unannounced nationwide rolling blackouts affecting ~2\u202fmillion households and industrial users.\u201d (doc_id\u202f=\u202f`trigger_34_korea_electric_power_2011`).  \n\n  This is the only concrete source confirming that a **nationwide rolling blackout** occurred on the date in question and that **industrial users were impacted**.\n\n* **Company\u2011specific filing** \u2013 A request for POSCO\u2019s own SEC filing (`get_supplier_info`) returned its 20\u2011F (24\u202fJun\u202f2011). The document contains no reference to the September\u202f2011 KEPCO outage, so it does not confirm any direct impact on POSCO.\n\n### What could **not** be confirmed\n\n* No individual downstream company (POSCO, Hyundai Motor, Kia, LS\u202fElectric, Samsung C&T, etc.) mentions the September\u202f2011 blackout in its own SEC filing or other disclosed material.  \n* Tier\u20112 firms (e.g., chemical producers, battery makers, automotive parts suppliers) likewise have no searchable mention of the event in their filings.  \n* The supply\u2011graph lists **107 enriched companies**; only a subset is shown here because they are the most recognizable industrial users. For the remaining dozens of tier\u20111 and tier\u20112 firms (e.g., Lucky Cement, Vedanta, various steel\u2011tube makers, etc.) we have no specific evidence of impact beyond the generic statement that \u201cindustrial users\u201d were affected.\n\n### Bottom line\n\n- The **structural risk** from KEPCO\u2019s September\u202f2011 rolling blackouts extends to a large set of downstream industrial customers.  \n- The only concrete evidence available is the **KEPCO event notice** itself, which confirms that *industrial users* suffered power interruptions.  \n- No company\u2011specific filings (including POSCO\u2019s 20\u2011F) provide direct confirmation that any particular downstream firm experienced material disruption or financial impact from this outage.  \n\nThus, while we can reasonably flag all tier\u20111 and tier\u20112 companies identified by the supply\u2011graph as **potentially affected**, the investigation remains **incomplete** with respect to company\u2011level verification of actual impact.\n\nCOVERAGE GAPS (106) -- reported explicitly, not omitted:\n  - lucky cement: no evidence gathered\n  - kumho petrochemical: no evidence gathered\n  - hyundai engineering & construction: no evidence gathered\n  - sk: no evidence gathered\n  - samsung c&t: no evidence gathered\n  - ls electric: no evidence gathered\n  - perusahaan perseroan persero pt perusahaan listrik negara: no evidence gathered\n  - hyundai rotem: no evidence gathered\n  - vedanta: no evidence gathered\n  - hansol chemical: no evidence gathered\n  - dongsung chemical: no evidence gathered\n  - hero moto